1
00:00:02,120 --> 00:00:07,380
Copilot agents are reusable mini-assistants you can configure for a specific workflow.

2
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:12,300
Instead of re-explaining the same task every time you open a new chat, you can give the

3
00:00:12,300 --> 00:00:17,500
agent a clear role, standing instructions, approved knowledge sources, and optional capabilities.

4
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:21,240
Think of it as turning a super prompt into a reusable assistant.

5
00:00:21,780 --> 00:00:27,720
You can create agents from the Microsoft 365 Copilot app, OneDrive, or SharePoint, depending

6
00:00:27,720 --> 00:00:30,620
on your license, permissions, and organization settings.

7
00:00:31,180 --> 00:00:36,320
For this demo, I'll use the most common path, the Microsoft 365 Copilot app.

8
00:00:36,820 --> 00:00:39,700
To begin, I go to the left side panel and click New Agent.

9
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:45,180
Here, I can either describe the agent I want to create in the prompt box, or skip directly

10
00:00:45,180 --> 00:00:45,860
to configuration.

11
00:00:46,700 --> 00:00:49,420
Since I already know what I want to build, I'll skip to configuration.

12
00:00:50,120 --> 00:00:54,440
In this example, I'll build an assistant that helps me write weekly leadership updates.

13
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:55,900
I'll name it Larry.

14
00:00:56,400 --> 00:00:58,020
Then, I add a short description.

15
00:00:58,580 --> 00:01:00,700
Helps me write weekly leadership updates.

16
00:01:01,580 --> 00:01:03,080
Next, I configure the agent.

17
00:01:03,720 --> 00:01:07,560
This brings me to the most important part, the instructions field.

18
00:01:08,280 --> 00:01:12,480
This is where I paste my tested prompt template, personalized, to my workflow.

19
00:01:13,180 --> 00:01:18,340
For this example, the instructions include four consistent elements, aligned with C.O.

20
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:23,600
Do super prompting, the role of the assistant, the objective of the workflow, the do's and

21
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:27,180
don'ts, and the output format I want every time.

22
00:01:27,880 --> 00:01:29,760
The fifth element is the variable part.

23
00:01:30,360 --> 00:01:34,740
That is what I provide each time I use the assistant, such as this week's notes, priorities,

24
00:01:35,060 --> 00:01:36,720
metrics, or meeting context.

25
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:40,660
The goal is not to write a long prompt every time.

26
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:45,340
The goal is to write it once, test it, improve it, and make it reusable.

27
00:01:46,180 --> 00:01:48,440
Then I can choose the knowledge sources.

28
00:01:48,440 --> 00:01:54,920
Agent builder can use sources like SharePoint, OneDrive, websites, teams, and approved connectors,

29
00:01:55,160 --> 00:01:57,020
depending on your organization's settings.

30
00:01:57,680 --> 00:01:58,940
The practical rule is simple.

31
00:01:59,300 --> 00:02:01,160
Only add sources that improve the workflow.

32
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:06,020
Too much context can create noise, contradictions, or outdated references.

33
00:02:06,700 --> 00:02:10,320
For this leadership update assistant, I'll keep it prompt-based first.

34
00:02:10,740 --> 00:02:13,020
After that, I review the agent's capabilities.

35
00:02:13,620 --> 00:02:17,480
Depending on your setup, you may be able to enable features like document creation,

36
00:02:17,480 --> 00:02:19,660
charts, code, or images.

37
00:02:20,220 --> 00:02:26,380
I'll enable document creation, so the final output can be turned into a word document more easily.

38
00:02:26,940 --> 00:02:28,500
I can also add starter prompts.

39
00:02:29,140 --> 00:02:32,720
This is optional, but useful if you want to share the agent to others.

40
00:02:33,320 --> 00:02:37,820
For Larry, I might add, draft this week's leadership update for my notes.

41
00:02:38,540 --> 00:02:42,020
Turn these project updates into an executive-ready summary.

42
00:02:42,700 --> 00:02:45,620
Rewrite this update to make it clearer and more concise.

43
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:51,680
These prompts help people understand what the agent is for without needing a separate explanation.

44
00:02:52,140 --> 00:02:55,600
And before I finalize it, I choose Preview to test the agent.

45
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:58,140
I paste the variable part of my workflow.

46
00:02:58,680 --> 00:03:01,160
Here are my inputs for the upcoming leadership meeting.

47
00:03:01,480 --> 00:03:02,520
Please process them.

48
00:03:02,860 --> 00:03:06,960
The agent generates a draft leadership update, and now I review it.

49
00:03:07,320 --> 00:03:07,900
Is it clear?

50
00:03:08,300 --> 00:03:09,500
Is it executive-ready?

51
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:11,900
Does it follow the structure I asked for?

52
00:03:12,280 --> 00:03:14,760
Does it avoid details that are too tactical?

53
00:03:15,320 --> 00:03:18,420
If not, I can refine the instructions and test again.

54
00:03:19,040 --> 00:03:22,220
Since I'm satisfied with the result, I click on Create.

55
00:03:22,780 --> 00:03:27,740
By default, the agent starts as private, but sharing options may include your organization

56
00:03:27,740 --> 00:03:30,720
or specific users, depending on admin policy.

57
00:03:31,320 --> 00:03:34,660
Once created, the agent usually appears in the left-side panel.

58
00:03:35,020 --> 00:03:41,120
In some Microsoft 365 experiences, I can also call an agent by ad mentioning it in a new chat.

59
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:45,820
That lets me bring the agent into the flow of work instead of switching contexts.

60
00:03:46,560 --> 00:03:48,220
And finally, notes on availability.

61
00:03:49,140 --> 00:03:55,480
What you see may depend on your Microsoft 365 license, admin settings, and work or school

62
00:03:55,480 --> 00:03:55,980
environment.

63
00:03:56,640 --> 00:04:00,920
And for more advanced agents with automation and integrations, use Copilot Studio,

64
00:04:00,920 --> 00:04:04,440
which is only available for business or enterprise-level subscriptions.

